                    'en' => [
                        'soru' => 'Do aluminium gazebos rust?',
                        'cevap' => 'No—powder-coated aluminium profiles do not rust; the residual risks are scratches and coat cracking, both handled with light touch-up. Example: coastal and municipal projects favour aluminium for salt-spray and UV resistance, and a 6×3 unit covers 18 m² of public space without structural corrosion worries. Alternative path: if the coating is scratched, local touch-up paint is enough; you never chase hidden rust points the way old steel-railing builds require. Source: product summaries citing corrosion-resistant aluminium profile, powder coating, and salt/UV endurance. See the low-maintenance path in /en/rehberler/bakim and models under /en/urunler; warranty terms stay at 5 years on /en/garanti, with free surveys Mon–Sat 09:00–18:00. Practice note: aluminium profiles are sealed with electrostatic powder coating; corrosion resistance comes from the coat and the alloy alone. Coastal projects carry salt-spray and UV-stability notes in the product summary. Do not use abrasive steel wool—microfibre or a soft sponge only. The upkeep log stays simple: 1–2 washes a year, gutter checks, local touch-ups if needed. Powder-coat thickness and pre-texturing decide adhesion; coastal zones may get an extra protective layer or marine-grade profile. Corrosion cover on aluminium can be extended by a separate policy. Cleaners must be pH-neutral—ammonia glass sprays cause dulling. Coastal projects carry salt-spray and UV-stability notes in the product summary. Do not use abrasive steel wool—microfibre or a soft sponge only. The upkeep log stays simple: 1–2 washes a year, gutter checks, local touch-ups if needed. Regular rinsing prevents moisture and salt build-up. There is no paint cycle on aluminium—only surface dulling may appear over years. Aluminium frames use screw-and-nylon joints rather than welded seams.',
