                    'en' => [
                        'soru' => 'How do you prevent mould on a gazebo?',
                        'cevap' => 'Yes — short summary (1 point): Prevent mould by leaving airflow gaps, keeping the base sweep clean, and sticking to paint or impregnation on wood; choosing composite or aluminium in damp shade cuts risk further. Example: a 4×4 wood unit in north-facing tree shade stays mould-free with a base clean every six months and a coat every 2–3 years. Alternative path: early mould yields to a vinegar-based clean and full drying; if it returns, renew the paint film. Source: care-guide principles plus moisture/insect resistance notes in product summaries. Aluminium and composite do not host mould; on wood, shade management comes first. Step lists live in /en/rehberler/bakim and material trade-offs in /en/rehberler/malzeme-karsilastirma; site checks happen on the free survey, Mon–Sat 09:00–18:00. Practice note: moisture can be checked with a handheld meter; painting wood above roughly 20% moisture is not advised. Shade management means pruning or relocating the unit—if pruning is impossible, switch to composite or aluminium. The base on concrete or compacted aggregate should shed water with a mild slope (about 2%). Leaf removal before winter cuts mould risk. When mould first appears the area is sealed locally; wide coverage needs a survey before any structural worry. If no moisture source is found, the shade tree is pruned or the unit relocated. On wood, suspect rot gets a section check; if core decay shows, part replacement opens. Warranty exclusions are listed on /en/garanti. During periodic checks connection nuts get a contact review every 6 months.',
